{
  "id": "660C",
  "role": "recipe-author",
  "notAnAudit": true,
  "validated": false,
  "statusNote": "Recipe author exclusion only. Not a qualification, audit, or signoff.",
  "semantics": "n, k (1 <= n <= 3*10^5, 0 <= k <= n), a_1..a_n in {0,1}. Print z = max length of an all-ones run after changing at most k zeros to ones, then ANY resulting array b achieving f(b) = z; several arrays may be valid and every one is accepted.",
  "exclusion": {
    "excluded": true,
    "category": "multiple-valid-outputs",
    "reasons": [
      "The original statement (codeforces.me div, bound below) says: 'On the second line print n integers aj — the elements of the array a after the changes.' and 'If there are multiple answers, you can print any one of them.' The pinned row's output_format carries the same sentence. Codeforces judges 660C with a checker that accepts any array b with b >= a pointwise, at most k zero-to-one changes, and f(b) = z. pipeline/checker.py and the repo judge compare whitespace-split tokens against one stored answer, and run_pool.py line 127 writes checkerSemantics '... Token comparison is appropriate; no floating/multiple-answer/special checker.' That claim would be false for 660C.",
      "The stored answers are not unique in the pinned official suite. 4 of the 21 pinned official tests (0-based indices [1, 6, 13, 19]) and sample index [1] have more than one valid output array. For example official test 1 / sample 2 ('10 2 / 1 0 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 1', z=5) stores '1 0 0 1 1 1 1 1 0 1' but '1 0 0 1 0 1 1 1 1 1' is equally valid. Official test 6 has 3 valid arrays and official test 13 ('3 1 / 0 0 0', z=1) accepts '1 0 0', '0 1 0' and '0 0 1'. Every count was confirmed by exhaustive enumeration over all <=k zero-flip subsets (all 21 official tests and both samples have n <= 10).",
      "Pinned CF-accepted sources use different tie-breaks, so a token checker would reject a real full-testset AC program. Sources were inspected, not executed. 17232829 (OK, passedTestCount 38 = testset_size 38, source sha256 79bdbf3197b7ed66a1d9f1d9b000239b4395791be8d1ec2e8c59e878271000d8) updates with 'if(R-L>=MR-ML) MR=R,ML=L;' at source line 31, so among optimal windows it keeps the LAST left end. A Python re-implementation of lines 27-36 (scratch, not the submission bytes) gives token-unequal but valid outputs on official tests [1, 6, 13, 19] (e.g. '1 0 0 1 0 1 1 1 1 1' on official test 1 and '0 0 1' on official test 13). 17233039 (OK, 38) uses strict improvement at line 16 ('if (i - l + 1 > ans)') and matches every stored output, so the stored suite reflects one tie-break, not a canonical answer.",
      "Forcing a unique answer (for example the leftmost optimal window) would make token comparison falsely unique: correct programs with another tie-break would be scored WA, and those verdicts are tie-break differences, not algorithmic proof. The pinned row has generated_checker=None, so open-r1 supplies no checker either. The task's exclusion rule for multiple-valid-output / custom-checker problems applies, so no recipe_660C.py or test_recipe_660C.py was written."
    ],
    "reversal": "Feasible only if the parent adds an audited custom-checker contract for 660C. The checker would parse z and n tokens b_j in {0,1}, require b_j >= a_j, at most k positions with b_j != a_j, the longest ones-run of b equal to z, and z equal to the optimum from a two-pointer/prefix-count oracle. Stored outputs would then serve only as witnesses. Every pinned official output and sample is valid (see officialReview), so no test bytes would need rewriting."
  },
